==Pestilent Bloat==
[[File:Fighting Pestilent Bloat.png|thumb|A group of players fighting The Butcher's crazed creation, the Pestilent Bloat.]]
The [[Pestilent Bloat]] is an [[undead]] abomination made from various body parts, and is the second boss in the Theatre of Blood.

Being undead, the [[salve amulet]] is very powerful against Bloat. Bloat is highly resistant to magic and ranged attacks and weak to melee, especially slash attacks. Before entering, make sure to equip the salve amulet and eat an anglerfish; otherwise, drop a potion.

===Movement mechanics===
When active (moving), the Bloat attacks anyone in its line of sight with flies, dealing up to 20 damage every [[tick]], reduced by 25% if [[Protect from Missiles]] are active. The flies can spread onto other players if the attacked player(s) have a line of sight on other player(s), even if the Bloat cannot directly see those players. Additionally, any damage dealt to the Bloat while active is halved (rounded down).

After a period of movement (around 20-25 seconds), the boss will stop and shut down for 32 game ticks (19.2 seconds). On the 30th tick of inactivity, the boss will stomp on the ground, dealing 40-80 damage and restoring any drained Defence levels. It is possible to [[Tick manipulation|tick eat]] this attack.

Bloat walks around the room in a fixed path around the central tank. On entering the Bloat area, the direction he will be walking will be random, but the position he spawns in is predictable. At the start of the fight, the bloat will always be walking and is unable to turn for 19.2 seconds. When pushed below 90% health, hands will begin to fall from the ceiling while he's active; where they land is indicated by looming shadows on the ground. Getting hit by falling hands will deal roughly 30-50 damage and stun the player for a few seconds.

When the Bloat is between 40% to 60% of its health, it will always run, moving two tiles at a time. Below 40%, it can either run or walk, alternating between walking and running for every hit made on it upon falling below 40% health. Therefore, it is recommended that teams avoid this threshold whenever possible, since the run can cause a lot of problems.

Additionally, the Bloat can abruptly change directions; there is a 32 tick cooldown (19.2 seconds) on these directional changes. On entering the room, the cooldown is 32. This timer is preserved between walks, and only counts down while bloat is walking.

The Bloat will naturally walk for between 34 and 42 ticks, unless it was not attacked by any player on the previous down, which extends the walk by 4 ticks. This means that on entering the room, Bloat will walk for between 38 and 46 ticks before going down, but subsequent walks will last 34 to 42. However, for 5 ticks following a change in direction, Bloat can not go down. This can cause walk durations longer than the usual maximum.

===Strategy===
[[File:Pestilent Bloat - BGS run-by.mp4|thumb|400px|A demonstration of a Bandos godsword run-by, draining 88 Defence levels from the Pestilent Bloat.]]
To start the fight have one raider enter the room when Bloat is on the opposite side of the pillar. Hug the pillar and hide from Bloat as it walks around the room. While optional, one or two players should do a run-by on the boss with a Bandos godsword special to lower its Defence, making it easier to hit as it has moderate defence bonuses.

As soon as Bloat deactivates, the rest of the team should enter and begin attacking with melee. The team will have roughly 18 seconds to deal damage before it reactivates. This allows for five attacks when close, or four from a distance. If using [[burning claws]], dump one special at the start of the attack rotation, as it clears [[burn]]s upon activating. The last hit in the four or five attack sequence should always be with a [[crystal halberd]] special, if enough energy is allowed to do so. Unless the boss is below 3% health, it is recommended to run away after the last attack, since stomps and flies have a high chance of killing a player.

Before entering the next room, raiders should drop their [[salve amulet (e)]] and buy a [[stamina potion(4)]] from the chest. If the mage and range role have high enough [[Agility]] This may not be necessary for them. If the party has a raider with [[potion share]] spells, only they need to purchase one, and share the stamina when needed.

